        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">The Leap Plus ships with a standard cylinder that stops at 19.5" — lower than the Steelcase Gesture (21") and the Herman Miller Aeron Size C (20.5"). Steelcase offers an optional 5" cylinder (about $63) that raises the ceiling to 22.5", the highest of the three, while also raising the floor to 17.5". If you are above roughly 6'2", you need to specify that cylinder when you order.</p>
      </div>

      <section>
        <h2>Leap Plus Seat Height: The Spec Explained</h2>
        <!-- tca-aio-capsule -->
        <p>TCA's Steelcase Leap Plus seat height page notes that the chair ships with a standard cylinder stopping at 19.5 inches — lower than both the Steelcase Gesture at 21 inches and the Herman Miller Aeron Size C at 20.5 inches. Steelcase offers an optional 5-inch cylinder, around $63, that raises the ceiling to 22.5 inches, the highest of the three. TCA treats that cylinder as required rather than optional for users at 6 feet 4 inches and above.</p>
        <p>The Steelcase Leap Plus (462 Series) has a standard seat height range of <strong>15.5 inches to 19.5 inches</strong> — measured from the floor to the top of the seat cushion. That is a 4-inch range, and it is what you get in the default configuration. Steelcase's specification guide also lists an <strong>optional 5" cylinder</strong>, which shifts the range to <strong>17.5 inches to 22.5 inches</strong> for roughly $63 at order time.</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">Source: <a href="https://www.steelcase.com/content/uploads/2020/10/Seating-Leap-Seat.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Steelcase Seating Specification Guide — Leap (PDF) →</a></p>
        <p>The distinction matters more than it looks. The optional cylinder does not simply extend the range upward — it moves the whole range up by 2 inches, so the minimum rises from 15.5" to 17.5". You are choosing between a low chair and a tall chair, not buying extra travel.</p>
        <p>For context: most standard office chairs max out at 17"–18". The Herman Miller Aeron Size C reaches 20.5" and has no tall-cylinder option. The Steelcase Gesture reaches 21" with its standard cylinder, or higher with an optional dealer-installed tall cylinder. A default Leap Plus reaches 19.5" — <em>below</em> both. A Leap Plus with the 5" cylinder reaches 22.5" — above both.</p>
        <p>For comparison within the Leap family, the standard Steelcase Leap adjusts 15.5"–20.5" as standard (a 5" range) and 17"–24" with its optional cylinder. At default configuration the Leap Plus is actually an inch <em>lower</em> at maximum than the regular Leap. The Plus trades seat-height range for a wider seat, a taller back, and a 500 lb weight capacity — not for height.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Why Seat Height Is the Foundation of Ergonomic Fit</h2>
        <p>Seat height determines everything else about how a chair fits. The ergonomic goal is simple: thighs parallel to the floor (or angled slightly downward), feet flat, knees at roughly 90 degrees. When this is wrong, the rest of the chair's adjustments can't compensate.</p>
        <p>For tall users, seat height is typically the first point of failure in standard chairs. A chair that maxes out at 17"–18" cannot be set up correctly for a 6'2" body — the seat is too low, the knees rise above the hips, the pelvis tilts backward, and the lumbar spine rounds under load. No lumbar pad fixes this. The chair simply doesn't fit.</p>
        <p>This is exactly why the cylinder choice on the Leap Plus is not a minor line item. A 6'5" buyer who orders the default configuration gets a 19.5" ceiling and reproduces the same failure mode in a $1,595 chair. With the 5" cylinder, the 22.5" ceiling removes it. Same chair, same price bracket, completely different fit outcome.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Recommended Seat Height by User Height</h2>
        <p>These are starting points based on average proportions. Your actual inseam length and desk height determine the precise setting. The final column shows which cylinder you need to reach that setting.</p>
      </section>

