    <p class="text-xs text-muted-foreground mb-8">Desk height ranges assume forearms parallel to floor. Adjust based on your actual desk and inseam length. Cylinder ranges per the <a href="https://www.steelcase.com/content/uploads/2020/10/Seating-Leap-Seat.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="underline">Steelcase Seating Specification Guide</a>.</p>

    <div class="prose-tca">

      <section>
        <h2>How to Adjust Seat Height on the Steelcase Leap Plus</h2>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Sit in the chair</strong> with your feet flat on the floor.</li>
          <li><strong>Locate the height adjustment lever</strong> — on the right side of the seat, below the right arm.</li>
          <li><strong>To raise the seat:</strong> Pull the lever up while slightly lifting your weight off the seat. The pneumatic cylinder extends.</li>
          <li><strong>To lower the seat:</strong> Pull the lever up while sitting fully in the chair. Your weight compresses the cylinder.</li>
          <li><strong>Release the lever</strong> at your target height.</li>
          <li><strong>Verify your position:</strong> Thighs parallel or angled slightly downward. Feet flat. Knees at roughly 90 degrees. Two to three fingers of clearance between the seat edge and the backs of your knees.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>At heavier body weights, the pneumatic cylinder compresses faster when lowering. If you overshoot your target height going down, raise it slightly and try again.</p>
        <p>If your chair tops out well short of where you need it, check which cylinder you have before assuming the chair is wrong for you. A standard-cylinder Leap Plus stops at 19.5". Cylinders are replaceable through a Steelcase dealer, but specifying the 5" cylinder at order time is far simpler than retrofitting.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Leap Plus vs Competitors: Seat Height Comparison</h2>
        <p>The three top chairs for tall users compared on seat height:</p>
        <h3>Maximum seat height side-by-side</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Steelcase Leap Plus, standard cylinder:</strong> 15.5"–19.5" (default configuration)</li>
          <li><strong>Steelcase Leap Plus, optional 5" cylinder:</strong> 17.5"–22.5" (about $63, specified at order)</li>
          <li><strong>Steelcase Gesture:</strong> 16"–21" (standard); higher with optional tall cylinder from dealer</li>
          <li><strong>Herman Miller Aeron Size C:</strong> 16"–20.5" (hard limit; no tall cylinder option)</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">Leap Plus figures per the <a href="https://www.steelcase.com/content/uploads/2020/10/Seating-Leap-Seat.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Steelcase Seating Specification Guide (PDF)</a>.</p>
        <p>For users 6'0"–6'3", all three chairs provide adequate seat height for standard desk setups, and a standard-cylinder Leap Plus is fine. The gap between them is small enough that seat height alone shouldn't drive the decision — breathability, seat depth adjustability, and armrests are more meaningful differentiators at this height range.</p>
        <h3>When the Leap Plus advantage kicks in — and when it doesn't</h3>
        <p>For users 6'4"+, a correctly specified Leap Plus has the best ceiling available: 22.5" with the 5" cylinder, 1.5" above the Gesture and 2" above the Aeron. That is a real advantage and it is the reason the Leap Plus keeps getting recommended for very tall users.</p>
        <p>But the advantage is entirely conditional on the cylinder. Ordered in its default configuration, the Leap Plus stops at 19.5" — lower than the Gesture and lower than the Aeron Size C, both of which reach their ceilings with no options at all. A 6'5" buyer who clicks through a generic listing without checking the cylinder ends up with the worst of the three chairs on the one spec they bought it for.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Desk Height Pairing for Tall Users</h2>
        <p>Seat height and desk height are interdependent. As your required seat height increases, your desk height needs to rise proportionally to keep your forearms roughly parallel to the floor (the standard ergonomic arm position for typing).</p>
        <h3>Recommended desk height by user height</h3>
        <p>Standard fixed desks are 28"–30" tall — fine for users 5'8"–6'1" but often too low for tall users sitting at higher seat heights. If you're 6'4"+ and need your Leap Plus seat at 20"+, you need both the optional 5" cylinder and a height-adjustable sit-stand desk in the 30"–34" range to dial in both settings correctly. Note that the optional cylinder's 17.5" floor is higher than the standard cylinder's 15.5", so a chair configured for tall use gives up some low-end range — worth knowing if the chair is shared or if you sometimes want it low.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Verdict: Is the Leap Plus Seat Height Range Enough?</h2>
        <h3>For users 6'0"–6'3"</h3>
        <p>The standard 15.5"–19.5" range covers you. Most users in this band need 17.5"–19.5", which sits inside the default configuration. Seat height will not be a constraint and you do not need to pay for the optional cylinder.</p>
        <h3>For users 6'4"–6'6"</h3>
        <p>The Leap Plus is the strongest choice on seat height <em>only if</em> you order the optional 5" cylinder. That configuration reaches 22.5", above anything the Gesture or Aeron Size C offer as standard. Ordered with the standard cylinder, the Leap Plus is the wrong chair for you at this height — its 19.5" ceiling will leave you sitting too low.</p>
        <h3>For users 6'7"+</h3>
        <p>Even with the optional cylinder, the 22.5" ceiling starts to approach your limit. Many users at 6'7" can still set up correctly at 22.5" depending on inseam length and desk height, but there is no margin left. At 6'8" and above, dedicated tall-user seating or custom solutions are worth investigating alongside the Leap Plus. The standard cylinder is not a viable option at any point in this range.</p>
      </section>

